              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The completed quote is: \n\n> **\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\nThis famous statement is attributed to **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406), the pioneering 14th-century Arab sociologist, historian, and philosopher, from his monumental work ***The Muqaddimah*** (*The Introduction*, written in 1377). \n\n### The Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun deeply analyzed the lifecycle of civilizations, empires, and economies. In the section where this concept is explored, he argued against the state or ruling elites engaging in commerce. \n\nAccording to Ibn Khaldun, when wealthy rulers or governments enter the marketplace as competitors, they inevitably distort it:\n* **Unfair Competition:** Rulers have access to immense capital, tax exemptions, and coercive power, making it impossible for ordinary merchants to compete on a level playing field.\n* **Market Destruction:** State-run commercial enterprises often force private merchants to buy goods at inflated prices or sell their goods to the state at artificially low prices, which ultimately kills private initiative and halts economic growth.\n* **The Power of the Private Merchant:** In contrast, private merchants who are \"responsible and organized\" must rely on efficiency, customer satisfaction, financial discipline, and prudent risk management to survive. \n\nIbn Khaldun asserted that while a ruler's business might look powerful initially due to sheer wealth, it is structurally inefficient and prone to corruption. In the long run, the disciplined, competitive, and highly organized businesses of private merchants will prove more resilient, adaptable, and ultimately **surpass** state-run enterprises.\n\n### Modern Relevance\nThis quote is often studied in economics and political science (and has famously featured as an essay prompt for the prestigious All Souls College Fellowship Exam at Oxford) as a brilliant, early critique of state capitalism. \n\nCenturies before Adam Smith wrote *The Wealth of Nations*, Ibn Khaldun laid down the foundational arguments for free-market economics, demonstrating how private enterprise, when guided by personal responsibility and organization, is far more sustainable and productive than top-down economic control by the state.",
